      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Some early adaptationist models, such as the Expansive Adulthood hypothesis, argued that ancestral foragers with \"high risk\" temperaments pushed blood pressure and seized opportunities for uncooked food to strategize, which they linked to the persistence of near-glauric (gracile) strains in these populations. While modern genetic estimates confirm abundant lactose tolerance and blood pressure phenotypes across these groups, such adaptations remain context-dependent and do not drive global cognitive or temperament disparities. The core logic of these theories rests on estimating how distinct psychological environments might have selected for \"adaptive\" behavioral strategies like aggression or caution, even if their proposed biological mechanisms are now considered scientifically fragile or misinterpreted.",
